<p>President Trump said he would review a new Iranian proposal to end the war but signaled he was unlikely to accept it. Two semiofficial Iranian news agencies, Tasnim and Fars, said Tehran sent a 14-point plan through a mediator in response to a nine-point U.S. proposal; Iran’s state-run media had not reported the proposal, according to AP reporting carried by KSTP and Fortune. The United States and Israel launched the conflict in late February, and hostilities have been on hold since April 8 after one failed round of peace talks. Reuters separately reported that a senior Iranian official described a phased offer addressing maritime access before nuclear issues. Trump told reporters in Florida he had been briefed on the “concept of the deal,” was awaiting the exact wording and could restart strikes if Iran “misbehaves”.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/ok6pvkU0oh9Vtl2EoxSENtAFNQ5NnDTFQz36FaY2rIlBwh5NBSl0IXVCQB643inO3cLFE3FVMkWbyh5L-IjNc3f-cqTx55-prRoyFucwgkriXWk8rTRHg0VS85hzsiSK2TEYDofPZ_DGEIn_CSyCHNdsKrIvxe4GVyBLWQ6C7q8' alt='President Donald Trump talks to reporters before boarding Air Force One at Palm Beach International Airport in West Palm Beach, Fla., on May 2, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Unclear details: The details of the latest plan were unclear, and Trump did not specify his objections, according to The New York Times.</li><li>Ceasefire status: AP reported that conversations had continued and that the three-week ceasefire appeared to be holding.</li><li>War warning: A senior military officer in Tehran said renewed fighting was “likely,” according to Dhaka Tribune and a Reddit-linked summary of Philippine coverage.</li></ul><blockquote>I will soon be reviewing the plan that Iran has just sent to us, but can’t imagine that it would be acceptable in that they have not yet paid a big enough price for what they have done to Humanity, and the World, over the last 47 years - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>P